module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb },

  receipts: [
    "**THE EMITTED REALIZATION REFUSES, AT PARSE, A SOURCE THE GRAMMAR ACCEPTS AND THE INTERPRETER PARSES** (INVALID STATE: one source text is fed to TWO realizations of the SAME canonical .dag parse function -- parse_module_prepared_seeded -- and they give DIFFERENT verdicts. Under the interpreter (the native test route, native_test_context_from_ingest -> native_census_module_resolution) the text PARSES and refuses only later at LOWERING with else_less_if_unlowered. Under the emitted gen-1 binary (the native CLI acquisition route, acquisition_parsed -> parse_module_prepared -> parse_module_prepared_seeded) the SAME text refuses at PARSE with parse_g0_tokens_remain. The text is a module whose single fn body is an ELSE-LESS if followed by a tail statement: `fn f(c: Bool, i: Int) -> Int {` / `  if c { i }` / `  i` / `}` (the rtp_guard_fallthrough_source fixture, byte-identical across the three cc2-ceprobe runs, member file sha256 0f785394e803ce694370e7c917d7faad069b70bfabc2eb2167cd2160dcf409a8; emit.err sha256 837b2a71). The interpreter is RIGHT: the grammar production dag_grammar_if_block_form is `seq(fn_body, optional(if_else_tail))` (dag.dag:2414), so an else-less `if c { i }` is a valid block-form member and the trailing `i` is the next statement; the whole module parses and the else-less refusal belongs at lowering)",

    "**LOCALIZATION (decided by the built-run differential plus a read-complete over both realizations; the two surviving models are named, not yet split)** -- cc2-pp3 (gen-1 sha 9cca13d3 built at 7d2215af, srv2 run.log 4484949c): elseless REJECTED parse_g0_tokens_remain, sameline (`if c { i } i` ONE line) REJECTED identically, and twostat (`i` newline `i`, no if) PARSES and refuses later at lowering (body_lowering_reason_statement_precedes_without_binding). So the emitted statement repeat is FINE in general, the fault is NOT line-break dependent (same verdict one-line vs two-line), and it is specific to A STATEMENT FOLLOWING AN IF-BLOCK. THE LINE-BREAK/LAYOUT FAMILY IS CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION: grammar_expr_after_line_break nodes exist at exactly two grammar sites (dag.dag:1795, the dual-role minus continuation guard, and dag.dag:1956, a bracket not-predicate) -- the if production and the statement sequence carry NONE -- and a layout-UNKNOWN answer would SET a LayoutUnknownWithoutSource refusal, while the emitted refusal is Absent. READ-COMPLETE: every function on both routes was read verbatim from the seed emission and compared to the .dag (fragments 1-13 of v2_compiler_parse.rs sha 188439f2: tokenizer/lexer/layout, sequence (no backtracking by design on BOTH sides), choice plans including token-prefix rows/select/conflicts and the compile_choice_plan/compile_class_bucket/choice_alts_* prep, optional, repeat and repeat_step, infix climb/continue/apply, not_predicate including its FIRST-gate, with_first refusal-gate, dispatch, the analysis fold family (nullable fixpoint, first rows, expr_first_fold, first_key_*), and the remainder check parse_production_unrefused) -- NONE diverges on read. What remains is RUNTIME DATA: per-production FIRST/nullable/plan instances computed by the emitted prepare_grammar at gen-1 runtime. Two models survive all measurements: M1 RECORD-SWALLOW -- the condition c greedily reads `c { i }` as a single-pun record literal (dag_grammar_record_field_init_list_helper accepts a bare pun; the only anti-record guard, dag_grammar_block_of_one_name_before_else_expr at dag.dag:1985, requires `else` after the `}` and cannot fire in an else-less if), so the if never parses; predicts REJECTED on BOTH executors for a block-final `if c { i }` with no tail. M2 IF-HANDBACK -- the if parses and the statement after its block is dropped by the emitted table handback; predicts ACCEPTED for that fixture. The namespace_xl0 controls all use `{ return i }` blocks, where the pun guard (value_name_guard refuses reserved names) blocks the record reading, so no green claim has ever exercised the swallow; the arbiter is ce_probe_blockfinal_text (member sha256 fce23fc1) in the cc2-pp6 row",

    "**WHAT MAKES IT RECURRING** (one shared emitted realization -- not one grammar rule): every statement of every module reaches the SAME emitted combinator realizations built by the seed emitter (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) from the .dag combinators, and the per-parse routing DATA (choice plans, FIRST rows, nullable sets) is produced by one emitted preparation run; a mis-emission in either is one class across the whole corpus, not one rule. A WHOLESALE break is self-excluded (the emitted compiler parses thousands of well-formed modules, and the read-complete found no combinator divergence), so the shape space is where a faithful combinator's decision flips on divergent runtime data -- which is why a shape rare in the corpus (a statement following an ELSE-LESS if whose block is a single bare identifier, usually block-final in real code) can stay latent in the self-host yet be a real defect",

    "**WHY IT IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF COVERAGE** (the interpreter is RIGHT, so no interpreted claim reddens -- the same blind spot the freemonoid list_init miscompile had, whose fix comment records 'THE INTERPRETER LOWERS ALL OF THESE CORRECTLY, which is why no interpreted claim discriminates the class'). A text-match emitted witness (compile_dag_rust_emit_check) CANNOT distinguish the two verdicts, because BOTH the wrong and the right realization compile and emit; per the nested_pattern row's N7-1 rule a text check 'cannot tell the two apart, so a probe written with it would be a decoration.' The only witness that discriminates is a BUILT-RUN differential: the same source bytes run through the interpreter AND the freshly-emitted gen-1 binary, asserting one parse verdict. That witness exists as the ce_probe_* family (elseless / twostat / sameline / elseelse / blockfinal plus the ce_probe_stmt_first_analysis prepared-analysis dump) on branch probe/jolly-carp-900-parse-path-unity, executed by the cc2 harness; the namespace_xl0 return_tail_position claims stand as the permanent interpreted-side controls, and per DESIGN section 353 the differential becomes a permanent regression control when the wall lands",

    "**RUNG FOUND AT** rung 1 -- one observed divergence between the two realizations on one real grammar input, reproduced three times on srv2 (cc2-ceprobe / ceprobe2 / ceprobe3, at main 0dc96e1e, seed gunbc 9e5ee601, gen-1 CLI 3ffe505e), with the interpreted control green (namespace_xl0 return_tail_position) and the emitted side red. No interpreter claim discriminates it, and no per-input built-run differential exists in the durable set yet",

    "**TRIGGER** the both-executor discriminating probe: build the seed gunbc, emit the gen-1, build the emitted crate, and run BOTH executors on the verbatim else-less member, expecting the SAME verdict (parse-OK -> refuse at lowering with else_less_if_unlowered). Red while the emitted realization is wrong; green once it matches the interpreter. Durable interpreted side: ce_probe_elseless_text in v2.test.claim.self_host.closure_emission.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE** wherever a construct is realized independently by the interpreter and by the emission target, ask what fact decides its behaviour and where that fact is DECLARED. For the grammar's combinators the deciding facts are the parse-result discriminants and the token classes the interpreter reads; any emitted arm or plan that decides on anything OTHER than those -- a first-token commit where the interpreter falls back, a field compare, an arm-order tie-break, an absent case that propagates instead of accepting -- is a candidate for this class. Do NOT special-case one grammar rule (do not name if_else_tail): the class is the shared emitted realization of a grammar combinator diverging from the interpreted .dag on one input.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_that_falls_through_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_as_the_last_statement_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
